

MOSAIC MULTICULTURAL CONNECTIONS 
Orien’s Story
Armidale, NSW  |  2026 
The Strength She Already Carried
Orien setting up morning tea for the women’s group, Newcastle 
On the 3rd of August, 2014, Orien Khalaf was given one instruction: go. 
ISIS  had  arrived in  Shingal.  Her  father, a  Yazidi policeman, had been  fighting  to hold  them 
back. At nine o’clock in the morning, he came running to their house. There were nine of them 
inside. He told them ISIS was following. They had to leave immediately. Her grandmother was 
too old to be able to walk to the mountain. She told them to go without her. They walked from 
nine in the morning until five in the afternoon. When they reached the mountain, they stayed. 
Seven days. No food. No water. Nothing. 
What  Orien  carried  off  that  mountain  is  not  the  kind  of  thing  you  can  see.  It  is  the 
understanding, forged in the most unimaginable circumstances, of what it means to be truly 
without — and to survive anyway. It is the knowledge of what it feels like to be a Yazidi woman 
in a world that has decided your life holds less value. And it is, quietly, a resolve that would 
take years to find its full shape. 
In the refugee camp, Orien Khalaf was the one who made the desserts. 
