She had siblings, and she had the knowledge that small things matter when the larger world 
has been stripped away. She taught herself to make pastries from whatever was available and 
brought something sweet to the people she loved. She was a teenager. She did it anyway. 
After four years in refugee camps, when Orien arrived in Australia in December 2018, she was 
safe.  But  safety,  as  many  newly  arrived  families  discover,  does  not  automatically  feel  like 
belonging. 
"We didn’t speak English. We didn’t know how to do simple things." 
She  found  a  job  at  a  local  café,  making  pastries.  It  was  a  beginning.  But  Orien  Khalaf  has 
never been someone content with only a beginning. She wanted to give back. 
Orien Khalaf, Thread Shed, Newcastle 
In 2019, Orien attended a Centrelink appointment. An interpreter had been booked for her. He 
spoke  Sorani,  a  Kurdish  dialect  different  from  her  own.  She  could  not  understand  him.  He 
could not understand why she did not understand. Help was being offered in that room, and 
none of it was reaching her. 
That  experience  did  not  leave  her. That was the moment  that  would  shape  everything  she 
became as a support worker. 
Orien first came to Mosaic to get her travel documents translated as part of the SETS Program, 
then again to seek assistance to fill out her application for her patisserie course at TAFE.  The 
work and workers of Mosaic made a deep impact on Orien and soon she began volunteering 
in Mosaic’s women’s group, sitting alongside women from many different backgrounds who 
did not speak English, translating and bridging what needed to be bridged. 

Orien at Mosaic’s women’s group, Newcastle 
“I made sure they really understood. I made sure they knew what was happening. 
Because I knew how it felt to be lost in translation.” — Orien Khalaf 
What  Orien  brought  to  that  volunteer  role  was  more  than  bilingual  ability.  It  was  lived 
experience of what it means to need help and not be reached. She was not simply filling a gap 
in  a  programme.  She  was  ensuring  that  the  women  she  sat  beside  were  never left  in  the 
position she had once found herself in. 
Mosaic recognised this. She was not a refugee who needed a service. She was someone with 
a deep, practical understanding of what good support looks like — and what its absence feels 
like too. She began as a volunteer. She grew into a role. 
Today, Orien works with Mosaic as a Bilingual Support Worker, supporting women aged 18 to 
35 through our domestic violence programmes. She is studying Certificate III in Community 
Services,  building  toward  a degree  in  social  work  at  the  University of  New  England and,  in 
time, working in women’s shelters. 
Last  year,  she  helped  a  woman  complete  her  barista  course.  She  filled  out  the  enrolment 
forms. She translated at the community college. She was there for every appointment. When 
the woman received her certificate, Orien felt it. 
“I was proud too. Because I remember when I did my patisserie course. It was 
hard, English wasn’t my first language, but I finished it.” — Orien Khalaf 
