Orien at Mosaic’s women’s group, Thread Shed, Newcastle 
That is  how  Orien  works.  She  does  not  point  people  toward  what  they  need  from  a  safe 
distance. She sits beside them, because she has walked the same road. 
When  ISIS  attacked  Shingal  in  2014,  they  came  in  part  because  Yazidi  women  and  girls 
could be  taken.  Because,  in  their  calculation,  being  a  Yazidi  woman  was  a  vulnerability. 
Orien  watched  that  happen.  It  shaped  every  professional  decision  she  has  made  since.  "I 
want to tell them now," she says. "You are strong. You can do anything you want." 
She says it as a statement of fact. Not comfort. Not reassurance. Fact. 
Orien has plans beyond what she is doing now. She wants to study social work. She wants, 
one  day,  to  open  her  own  patisserie  in  Paris.  She  wants  to  keep  building,  keep  learning, 
keep giving back to women who have seen what she has seen. 
“Mosaic didn’t just help my family settle. They helped me find my purpose. They showed 
me that my journey - the hard parts, the language struggles, everything I 
went through - 
it wasn’t just for me. It was so I could help other women find their strength too.” 
 — Orien Khalaf 
She wants to say thank you to Australia for safety and freedom. And thank you to Mosaic 
for seeing not just who she was when she arrived, but who she could become. 
The 
pastries she makes now are not for surviving. They 
are for celebrating. 
Orien's journey is a   reminder that behind every case is a person with strengths, ambitions 
and the potential to make a difference in the lives of others. 
Since opening our Armidale office in 2019, Mosaic has supported 743 clients across 
1,264 cases. 
In 2025-26 alone, we assisted 159 clients through 337 cases, helping people find not only 
safety and stability, but purpose and possibility.
